TITLE: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity: A Tutorial and Survey of Automated Vulnerability Discovery
TEXT: Received 28 May 2026, accepted 22 June 2026, date of publication 26 June 2026, date of current version 9 July 2026. Digital Object Identifier 10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3707573 LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity: A Tutorial and Survey of Automated Vulnerability Discovery ROBEL ADDIS, KIRAN PORTER, JAYDEN RYAN , MICAH STULL, SHENGJIE XU , (Senior Member, IEEE), PAUL WAGNER , ROBERT HONOMICHL , AND STEVEN FORSYTH College of Information Science, The University of Arizona, Tucson, AZ 85721, USA Corresponding author: Shengjie Xu (sjxu@arizona.edu) This work was supported in part by the Tides Foundation (Prime Sponsor: Google Inc.) ‘‘Supporting Communities and Small Business by Conducting Risk and Vulnerability Assessments through Student Experiential Learning Opportunities’’ under Award ID TF2405-120744, and in part by Arizona Cybersecurity Clinic at The University of Arizona. ABSTRACT This paper provides a tutorial and survey of LLM-based agents for automated vulnerability discovery and penetration testing. The rapid advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have opened new possibilities for their application in cybersecurity. Unlike traditional tools, LLMs can process natural language, retain context, and perform structured reasoning, enabling automation of complex security workflows. By equipping these models with memory mechanisms and tool-use interfaces, researchers have developed LLM-driven autonomous agents capable of performing specialized tasks such as web application testing, source code auditing, fuzzing, and network security assessment. Multi-agent architectures have emerged in which agents assume specialized roles (e.g., distinct penetration-test phases or exploit types). In addition to surveying existing applications, this work provides a step-by-step walkthrough of integrating agentic AI into penetration testing workflows. The walkthrough explores four phases: (I) mission scoping and prompt engineering for test definition and constraint enforcement, (II) autonomous exploration and tool selection for target interaction, (III) vulnerability hypothesis formation and verification through experiment design and feedback, and (IV) payload generation and refinement to transform validated findings into concrete exploits. The survey synthesizes 155 cited sources (2022–early 2026) using explicit inclusion/exclusion criteria, with coverage extending to reasoning-class models, the Model Context Protocol ecosystem, autonomous bug-bounty agents, and standardized agentic security benchmarks. Despite progress, challenges persist, such as model hallucination, context limitations, operating costs, and ethical concerns. Future directions include advancements in multi-agent collaboration, mechanisms for agent selfimprovement, and improved transparency into agent behavior. INDEX TERMS Large language models (LLMs), AI agents, cybersecurity, autonomous penetration testing, prompt engineering, vulnerability discovery, static application security testing (SAST), multi-agent systems, chain-of-thought (CoT), ReAct, Plan-then-Execute (PtE). I. INTRODUCTION A. CONTEXT AND MOTIVATION Cybersecurity operations today are defined by an unprecedented scale and growing complexity. Enterprise networks, The associate editor coordinating the review of this manuscript and approving it for publication was Turgay Celik . cloud infrastructures, and software supply chains evolve faster than conventional information security defensive measures can adapt. Classical approaches such as manual penetration testing, rule-based Static Application Security Testing (SAST), and procedural network scanning remain indispensable, yet they struggle to address the velocity and variety of modern threat landscapes [1], [2]. Prior 100884 2026 The Authors. This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License. For more information, see https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ VOLUME 14, 2026 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity work has explored automated penetration testing through other means, such as reinforcement learning for post-breach scenarios [3]. Recent advances in LLMs have introduced a new paradigm: intelligent agents capable of reasoning about context, interpreting code semantics, and orchestrating security toolchains autonomously. Research by Keltek et al. [1] on LLM-Supported SAST (LSAST) shows that coupling traditional static analyzers with LLM post-processors enables deeper semantic correlation across codebases, which is an ability unattainable in purely rule-driven systems. Likewise, Nakatani [4] and Luong et al. [2] demonstrate how multiagent penetration testing frameworks can coordinate reconnaissance and exploitation through adaptive orchestration, integrating tools such as Nmap and dnsenum with reasoning modules that interpret intermediate findings. LLM-assisted fuzzing for software security represents another emerging application [5]. These innovations mark the emergence of AI-augmented security intelligence, systems that replicate aspects of expert judgment by analyzing results, prioritizing vulnerabilities, and refining actions in real time. Frameworks like VulnBot [6] and xOffense [2] exemplify this evolution, embedding LLM reasoning within feedback-driven pipelines that accelerate discovery and reduce human oversight. What distinguishes this technological moment is not merely faster automation but a deeper cognitive integration between reasoning and action. As explored throughout this paper, LLMbased agents represent an emerging class of cybersecurity systems capable of traversing the entire reasoning loop, from prompt to payload, bridging perception, interpretation, and exploitation in a unified and adaptive process. B. PROBLEM DEFINITION AND GAP Despite rapid progress in AI-augmented cybersecurity, current research remains fragmented across specialized domains. Individual studies have focused on narrow capabilities, such as autonomous web exploitation, code auditing, or network scanning, but few have examined how these components integrate into a unified, reasoning-driven workflow. Traditional surveys on AI in cybersecurity typically emphasize model performance and dataset composition, with less emphasis on agentic process design; that is, how perception, reasoning, and action are coordinated through modular LLM architectures. This fragmentation leaves several open gaps. First, the absence of a shared reference architecture obscures how tools like VulnBot [6], RapidPen [4], AutoPentester [7], or LSAST [1] operationalize the reasoning loop from observation to execution. Second, limited interpretability and lack of benchmarking make it difficult to assess reliability across frameworks. Third, existing literature seldom explains how prompting techniques, reasoning chains (ReAct, Planthen-Execute), and structured workflows can be tuned for secure, verifiable automation. Consequently, practitioners and learners in the field face a growing need for an integrative resource that encompasses these three aspects: 1) Explaining the underlying mechanisms VOLUME 14, 2026 of LLM-driven agents, 2) Synthesizing the diverse experimental systems into a coherent taxonomy, 3) Establishing conceptual continuity between traditional and AI-driven security pipelines. This paper aims to address these gaps by offering both a tutorial foundation and a comparative survey of intelligent security agents. C. AIM AND SCOPE Recent systems have demonstrated impressive capabilities in isolated tasks such as navigating web applications, interpreting code semantics, or coordinating external security tools; however, integrated guidance that explains how these components operate together within a coherent agentic workflow remains scarce. The following paragraphs clarify the scope of this survey and the audience it serves. The scope of this survey focuses specifically on LLMpowered agents within cybersecurity, not on machine learning for cybersecurity as a whole. The intention is not to catalog every AI-based security tool. Instead, the paper examines how current agent architectures combine natural language reasoning, planning, memory mechanisms, and tool interaction to perform complex security operations with a degree of autonomy that was previously unattainable. By concentrating on these systems, the survey highlights the design patterns that shape their behavior, the operational loops that guide their decision-making, and the emerging conventions that define how they transform high-level objectives into actionable security outcomes. Beyond exploring existing approaches, this survey also serves as an educational foundation. Readers who are new to agentic AI will find a structured introduction to concepts such as chain-of-thought reasoning, ReAct (Reason + Act) style prompting, function calling, and multi-step planning. They will benefit from comparative analysis across application domains, as well as a detailed walkthrough of the complete workflow that connects prompts to payloads. Through this dual purpose, both tutorial and survey, the paper provides a cohesive view of the mechanisms, capabilities, and limitations of current LLM-based security agents. To sharpen differentiation from prior cybersecurity survey papers, this work centers on an operational lens: how LLMbased systems move from mission scoping to tool-mediated exploitation while preserving safety constraints, traceability, and verification checkpoints. The tutorial component is intentionally scoped to concepts directly needed for interpreting surveyed frameworks, while the comparative sections prioritize cross-framework synthesis and design trade-offs. D. SURVEY METHODOLOGY This survey follows a structured evidence-gathering process to improve transparency and reproducibility. The search process focused on peer-reviewed papers and high-quality preprints spanning approximately 2022 to early 2026, when modern agentic LLM security systems emerged rapidly. Query families included terms such as ‘‘LLM AI agents,’’ ‘‘Plan-and-Execute reasoning,’’ ‘‘tool use,’’ 100885 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity ‘‘function calling,’’ ‘‘penetration testing,’’ ‘‘vulnerability scanning,’’ ‘‘API penetration testing,’’ and ‘‘self-improving agents.’’ Inclusion criteria required direct relevance to LLM-based agent architectures, workflows, or evaluations for vulnerability discovery, penetration testing, source-code security analysis, or network-security operations. Exclusion criteria removed studies centered only on non-agentic ML classifiers, purely theoretical discussions without architectural or operational detail, duplicate reports of the same system without substantial updates, and works lacking enough methodological detail to support reliable comparison. Screening proceeded in three stages: (1) title/abstract filtering for domain fit, (2) full-text review for architectural and evaluation detail, and (3) subsection assignment by primary contribution (web/API testing, SAST/code auditing, network/infrastructure, or cross-cutting workflow/challenges). Because reported metrics are heterogeneous across papers, comparative tables retain source-reported values and explicitly mark missing fields as NR (Not Reported) instead of imputing estimates. The resulting corpus primarily informed three application subsections in Section III, with additional foundational and safety/governance references used in Sections II, IV, and V. This process provides a reproducible, auditable survey backbone while preserving flexibility for a fast-evolving literature. Following a PRISMA-style accounting (adapted for a literature survey), the search initially identified over 200 candidate records (based on deduplicated results across academic databases: IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, Scopus, Google Scholar, and curated preprint sources including arXiv cs.CR, cs.AI, cs.SE). Title/abstract screening retained approximately 160 records; full-text review retained 155 unique sources that are cited in this manuscript (the count grew modestly from the initial submission due to 2025–2026 developments added during revision). The retained references distribute approximately as follows: about 18–22 inform the LLM and agentic-foundation tutorial in Section II, about 28–34 inform the three application subsections in Section III (web/API testing, SAST and code auditing, network/infrastructure scanning), about 16–20 underpin the prompt-to-payload workflow in Section IV, and about 30– 40 ground the safety, governance, dual-use, and futuredirection discussion in Section V. Counts are approximate because a substantial subset of references is cited in more than one section (e.g., MAPTA, AutoPT, HPTSA, IRIS, VulnBot, xOffense, and PentestGPT recur across the survey, workflow, and challenges sections). This survey’s primary focus is offensive and evaluative agents for vulnerability discovery. Adjacent applications that share the same agentic substrate (malware reverse engineering, supply-chain package vetting as briefly discussed in Section III-B, defensive SOC automation, and LLM-based digital forensics) are acknowledged as promising extensions but are not surveyed comprehensively. 100886 E. STRUCTURE OVERVIEW After this introduction, Section II offers a tutorial-style explanation of the essential components that enable LLMbased agents to operate. It begins with the Large Language Model itself, including its handling of context, its instructionfollowing behavior, and its prompting strategies. The section then describes the agentic loop that links perception, reasoning, and action. It concludes by examining memory systems and tool-use interfaces, which together allow an agent to interact effectively with the external world. Section III transitions from foundational concepts to practical applications by surveying how LLM agents are currently used across major security domains. These domains include web application and Application Programming Interface (API) penetration testing, automated source code auditing, and network and infrastructure scanning. Each subsection highlights the common techniques, reasoning strategies, and operational patterns observed across existing frameworks. The section ends with a comparative analysis that distills recurring design choices, limitations, and differences across the approaches reviewed. Section IV provides a practical walkthrough of the full agent-driven security workflow, from mission scoping through payload refinement, demonstrating how high-level instructions become structured actions within an autonomous penetration testing lifecycle. Section V addresses the open challenges involved in deploying or developing LLM-based agents. It discusses technical issues such as hallucination, context length constraints, and cost considerations. It then examines broader safety and ethical concerns, including containment, dual-use risk, and the potential misuse of autonomous capabilities. The section concludes by outlining future research directions, including advances in multi-agent collaboration and mechanisms that promote transparency and self-improvement. Finally, the paper concludes by summarizing the central themes and emphasizing the significance of LLM-driven agents as a transformative step in automated cybersecurity operations. II. ANATOMY OF AN LLM-BASED SECURITY AGENT A. THE CORE ENGINE: THE LARGE LANGUAGE MODEL 1) CONTEXT WINDOWS A central concept in understanding how LLMs process information is the context window, which refers to the amount of text that a model can process at once during inference. In essence, the context window functions as the model’s working memory, enabling it to maintain coherence through sequential tokens and preserve dependencies in language generation [8]. Models such as GPT-4, Claude 3, and Llama 3 vary in their context window capabilities, with some extending to millions of tokens, as shown in Table 1. These extended windows allow LLMs to process longer sequences of text, which, in the context of this paper, are particularly useful for tasks in vulnerability discovery that involve analyzing extensive documentation, logs, or code. VOLUME 14, 2026 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity TABLE 1. Representative LLM context window specifications (approximate vendor-reported values, 2024–2026 reasoning-class models; not benchmark results). isolate salient information, thereby reducing sequence length without fully sacrificing context [22]. More recent architectural innovations such as SelfExtend introduce multi-level attention mechanisms that enable models to map unseen token positions into familiar ranges, effectively extending their usable context without additional fine-tuning [23]. Context windows represent both strengths and limitations for LLMs. They enable models to sustain reasoning over extended inputs, yet their fixed nature and susceptibility to performance decline with long text sequences require ongoing innovation. For LLM-based security agents, understanding and optimizing the context window is crucial, as the complexity of real-world vulnerability data often exceeds the reliable operating range of existing models. The 2024–2026 reasoning-model class (e.g., OpenAI o3/o4-mini, DeepSeek-R1, Claude 3.7/4 Sonnet, Claude 4.5/4.6 Opus, Gemini 2.5/3 Pro, Llama 4) introduces a qualitative shift relevant to security agents: these systems allocate substantial test-time compute to internal deliberation chains before responding, going beyond reliance on parameter scale alone [9], [10], [11], [12], [13], [14], [15], [16]. For penetration-testing agents, this trades latency and per-call cost for stronger multi-step planning and selfverification, which can directly reduce hallucinated exploit hypotheses (Section V-A1) and improve plan quality in longhorizon agent loops. Several surveyed frameworks (e.g., AutoPT, HPTSA, MAPTA) report sensitivity to underlying model capability [17], [18], [19]; with reasoning models, planner roles benefit disproportionately while bulk toolcalling executors can often remain on cheaper, faster models. The cost discussion in Section V-A3 is updated to reflect this asymmetric deployment pattern. However, empirical research shows that longer context does not always lead to better performance. Liu et al. [20] found that LLMs often exhibit a ‘‘lost in the middle’’ effect, where information located midway through long sequences is recalled less effectively than information at the beginning or end. Similarly, Levy et al. [21] demonstrated that model accuracy tends to plateau beyond a certain input length, indicating diminishing returns. These findings highlight that while extended context windows expand potential applications, they also introduce challenges such as performance degradation, higher computational costs, and Out-of-Distribution (OOD) effects related to positional encoding. To address these problems, se [...] pability, controllability, compliance constraints, and operating cost in enterprise deployments. Key open questions include optimal task decomposition strategies, communication protocols between agents, and mechanisms for resolving conflicts when specialized agents produce contradictory assessments. Distinct multi-agent failure modes also require explicit treatment: coordination overhead, tool-chatter loops, divergent plans across specialist agents, and verification bottlenecks when multiple agents produce partially conflicting evidence. Common mitigations include planner–executor role separation, bounded tool-call budgets, checkpointed replanning, and mandatory human approval gates for high-impact actions. As an extension of multi-agent collaboration, one intriguing direction is dedicated social-engineering support agents, for example, an agent that scrapes a target site and develops context-specific wordlists relevant to the target [74]. This alignment with real-world practice could improve realism, but it also amplifies ethical and safety risks that must be constrained by governance controls. An adjacent domain sharing the same agentic substrate is malware analysis and reverse engineering. AgentREBench benchmarks LLM agents on long-horizon reverse 100912 engineering tasks requiring chained tool calls (strings, objdump, debugger) to extract C2 infrastructure, encoding schemes, and anti-analysis techniques from synthetic ELF binaries [153]. TTPDetect combines dense and neural retrieval with an LLM agent to identify MITRE ATT&CK TTPs in stripped malware binaries, achieving approximately 93% precision and recall at function-level analysis [154]. These systems demonstrate that the ReAct/PtE patterns, structured memory, and tool-mediated verification examined throughout this survey transfer directly to defensive binary analysis, and represent a clear research extension direction. 2) SELF-IMPROVING AGENTS Another important direction is the ability for agents to learn from successes and failures. The utilization of external repositories to augment replanning capabilities has been used [2]. Some work assists agents in CTF-focused tests by providing them with examples of failed paths and subsequent successful ones [155]. However, several problems persist. One challenge is enabling agents to recover from faulty strategies and actions: research shows that some agents continually pursue faulty strategies without reconsidering current exploitation paths, or terminate early [17], [76], and that agents tend to fail as the number of reattempts increases [19], [155]. One possible solution is the implementation of a dedicated reflection component. RefPentester takes this approach with a dedicated ‘‘Reflector’’ that accounts for successful or failed execution and the correctness of high- and lowlevel knowledge, reflects on this information, and rewards successful operations and correct knowledge to improve performance [149]. Further research into these dedicated reflection mechanisms is important. Future work should also explore mechanisms for agents to automatically curate and retrieve relevant past experiences, distinguish between context-specific failures and generalizable lessons, and safely update their behavior without introducing regressions or security vulnerabilities in the learning process itself. 3) EXPLAINABILITY AND TRUST Designing agentic systems requires careful consideration of both explainability and overall trust. A simpler method to gain visibility is CoT prompting [25]. The requirement of agents to keep track of plan-related structures like PentestGPT’s PTT [74] or xOffense’s TCG [2] is another approach. Greater logging of agent activity (including tool use, reasoning, reflection, and memory) would help determine impacts and risks [148]. The activity of penetration testing also requires careful consideration of privacy associated with the data that agents may interact with, adding an additional layer of complexity [148]. Despite these challenges, agentic penetration testing systems should provide a level of auditability for trust and system improvement. Research priorities include developing standardized audit trail formats for security agent activities, creating interpretable summaries of multi-step reasoning chains, and establishing certification frameworks VOLUME 14, 2026 R. Addis et al.: LLM-Based Intelligent Agents for Cybersecurity that can validate agent behavior against organizational security policies before deployment. 4) LIMITATIONS OF THIS SURVEY Several limitations should be acknowledged when interpreting the findings presented here. First, the field is evolving rapidly; a substantial portion of the 2025–2026 literature is drawn from preprints and workshop papers that have not yet undergone full peer review and may be revised or superseded. Second, the scope intentionally centers on offensive and hybrid (offense-informed-defense) agentic systems; purely defensive agents, formal verification methods, and nonagentic ML classifiers for vulnerability detection are not comprehensively covered. Third, evaluation heterogeneity limits direct cross-framework comparison: surveyed systems differ in target environments, success metrics, trial counts, and threat models, as reflected by the ‘‘NR’’ (Not Reported) markers in Table 8. Finally, the literature search was conducted primarily across English-language databases (IEEE Xplore, ACM DL, arXiv, Google Scholar), which may underrepresent relevant work published in other languages. VI. CONCLUSION The greatest near-term impact of agentic AI on vulnerability discovery is likely to come from hybrid configurations in which agents act as copilots or first-pass explorers under human oversight. This survey has synthesized the emerging landscape of LLM-based security agents, from foundational mechanisms (context management, prompting, memory, and tool use) through surveyed frameworks spanning web/API penetration testing, source-code auditing, and network reconnaissance, to an operational four-phase workflow that bridges architectural patterns and deployment practice. The fourphase walkthrough in Section IV offers practitioners a concrete path to integrate agentic AI into penetration testing. Across domains, three recurring enablers distinguish effective systems: (i) containerized, auditable execution environments that constrain agent behavior [17], [76], (ii) structured planning representations (FSMs, task graphs, attack trees) that anchor reasoning and reduce hallucinated actions [2], [6], [74], and (iii) deterministic verification layers that ground LLM outputs in tool-confirmed evidence [1], [77], [78]. Persistent challenges remain: hallucination of plausible but non-existent vulnerabilities, context-window limitations during long-horizon engagements, escalating cost at scale, and unresolved governance questions around fully autonomous offensive tooling [20], [21], [108]. Three research priorities emerge from the gaps identified in this survey. First, standardized evaluation protocols that bridge controlled CTF benchmarks and production deployment conditions are needed to enable meaningful cross-study comparison. Second, containment and governance certification frameworks must mature so that organizations can deploy autonomous offensive agents with auditable risk envelopes. Third, cost-efficient tiered architectures, pairing reasoningclass planners with lightweight executors, require further VOLUME 14, 2026 study to scale agentic security testing within enterprise CI/CD pipelines without prohibitive expense. By placing agentic AI within this balanced outlook, the paper positions these systems as powerful but incomplete tools that can significantly accelerate vulnerability discovery when paired with clear operational boundaries, thoughtful architectural design, and knowledgeable human supervision. ACKNOWLEDGMENT (Robel Addis, Kiran Porter, Jayden Ryan, and Micah Stull contributed equally to this work.)

[R0740] year=2026 | venue=Journal of Cybersecurity and Privacy
TITLE: LLM-Based Agents for Cybersecurity: A Systematic Review of Architectures, Applications, and Open Challenges
TEXT: Systematic Review LLM-Based Agents for Cybersecurity: A Systematic Review of Architectures, Applications, and Open Challenges George Fatouros 1,* , Konstantinos Mavrogiorgos 2,* , Georgios Makridis 2 , John Soldatos 1 and Dimosthenis Kyriazis 2 1 Innov-Acts Ltd., 6 Kolokotroni Street, 1101 Nicosia, Cyprus; jsoldat@innov-acts.com 2 Department of Digital Systems, University of Piraeus, 18534 Piraeus, Greece; gmakridis@unipi.gr (G.M.); dimos@unipi.gr (D.K.) * Correspondence: gfatouros@innov-acts.com (G.F.); komav@unipi.gr (K.M.) Academic Editor: Martin Gilje Jaatun Received: 6 July 2026 Revised: 29 August 2026 Accepted: 6 September 2026 Published: 9 September 2026 Copyright: © 2026 by the authors. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland. This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license. Abstract The rapid evolution of Large Language Models (LLMs) has opened new frontiers in cybersecurity automation, enabling intelligent agents capable of multi-step reasoning, tool invocation, and autonomous decision-making across complex security tasks. While individual applications have emerged across threat intelligence, vulnerability assessment, penetration testing, and security operations center (SOC) automation, a systematic understanding of the LLM-based agent paradigm in cybersecurity—encompassing both single-agent and multi-agent architectures—remains lacking. This paper presents a systematic literature review following PRISMA guidelines, identifying records through 59 structured web-search queries whose results resolve predominantly to arXiv, Semantic Scholar, the ACM Digital Library, IEEE Xplore, USENIX, MDPI, SpringerLink, and Elsevier ScienceDirect, supplemented by citation chaining, for works published between January 2022 and April 2026; the full query record is published with the paper. We applied structured inclusion and exclusion criteria and classified 59 primary studies along five dimensions: security function, agent architecture pattern, knowledge augmentation strategy, human-in-the-loop posture, and evaluation rigor. Our analysis reveals that penetration testing and threat intelligence are the most extensively studied domains, while incident response and compliance verification remain critically underrepresented. Penetration testing alone accounts for over half the corpus (50.8%). Single-agent tool-calling remains the most prevalent architecture (30.5% of studies), whereas centralized multi-agent orchestration—present in 18.6%—yields the strongest reported performance gains, up to 4.3× on zero-day exploitation; prevalence and performance therefore point in opposite directions. No included study achieves production-grade (E4) evaluation: the entire field currently rests on controlled laboratory assessments. An independent search of six bibliographic databases recovers 86.3% of the studies the primary search had surfaced (79.7% of the full corpus) while indicating a total eligible literature of roughly 400 studies, so the corpus is reported as a documented subset rather than an exhaustive census. We propose a unifying taxonomy, identify cross-cutting challenges including hallucination, prompt injection, and benchmark fragmentation, and outline open research directions with particular emphasis on multi-agent orchestration design. Financial sector applicability under DORA and the EU AI Act is treated as a documented evidence gap rather than a synthesis: the corpus’s only compliance and risk assessment study is also its only banking-specific system. J. Cybersecur. Priv. 2026, 6, 159 https://doi.org/10.3390/jcp6050159 J. Cybersecur. Priv. 2026, 6, 159 2 of 50 Keywords: large language models; cybersecurity agents; multi-agent systems; LLM orchestration; threat intelligence; vulnerability assessment; penetration testing; retrievalaugmented generation; security operations; systematic review 1. Introduction The increasing volume, velocity, and sophistication of cyber threats have placed significant operational pressure on modern Security Operations Centers (SOCs), vulnerability management teams, and incident response units [1]. Traditional rule-based and signaturedriven defenses are proving insufficient against adaptive adversaries, leading to alert fatigue, delayed triage, and escalating remediation costs [2]. Concurrently, Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in natural language understanding, code reasoning, and multi-step planning [3,4], creating a compelling opportunity to redesign cybersecurity workflows around intelligent, autonomous agents. An LLM-based agent differs from a conventional LLM deployment in that it operates within an action loop: it perceives context, reasons over available tools, issues actions, observes outcomes, and iterates toward a goal [5,6]. In cybersecurity, this paradigm enables behaviors such as autonomous exploit chain construction, cyber threat intelligence (CTI) knowledge graph extraction, real-time alert triage, and adaptive incident response—tasks that previously required sustained expert engagement. Multi-agent extensions introduce further capability: specialized subagents operating under a central orchestrator can decompose complex security events into parallel, domain-specific analyses that a single generalist agent would otherwise have to perform sequentially [7]. Despite a growing body of individual system proposals, the field lacks a consolidated, methodology-driven synthesis of how LLM agents are being applied across the cybersecurity domain. Several recent surveys address adjacent ground. He et al. [8] treat the security and privacy of LLM agents as attack targets rather than as tools for cybersecurity work; Kaniewski et al. [9] focuses on a single application area, and Deng et al. [10]’s PentestGPT demonstrates what a single well-engineered system achieves in one function (penetration testing) rather than surveying the field. Closest to the present work, Srinivas et al. [1] apply a PRISMA-guided screening protocol to LLMs and agents for security automation with the security operations center as the organizing frame, and Shahriar et al. [11] document a comparably systematic search and screening process over a much larger, general agenticsecurity corpus; both stop short of publishing a per-paper coding matrix a reader can audit. Refs. [12–14] provide narrative overviews of agentic security published in 2025–2026. What distinguishes this review is summarized in Table 1. None of the adjacent surveys publishes a reader-auditable, per-paper coding matrix: Srinivas et al. [1] states its screening criteria and funnel but tabulates only representative systems rather than its full 105-paper corpus, and Shahriar et al. [11] publishes a continuously updated list of its 260-plus papers without per-paper dimensional coding. We instead apply a documented retrieval and screening protocol, publish the full per-paper coding matrix across five dimensions, and span eight security functions rather than a single operational context or sub-domain. We additionally analyze the regulatory surface—the Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA), the Network and Information Security Directive 2 (NIS2), and the EU AI Act—and audit retrieval-augmented systems for reporting completeness (Section 6), neither of which any adjacent survey treats. The contribution is therefore less the observation that LLM agents are being applied to security, which is by now uncontroversial, than a structured and auditable account of where, how, and how well. https://doi.org/10.3390/jcp6050159 J. Cybersecur. Priv. 2026, 6, 159 3 of 50 Table 1. Positioning of this review against the closest adjacent surveys. SLR: Systematic literature review. “Corpus published” indicates whether a per-paper classification of the reviewed works is provided in a form a reader can audit or extend. Scope Method Corpus published Architecture analysis HITL and evaluation rigor [1] SOC automation PRISMA-guided; stated inclusion criteria (105-paper funnel) No—representative systems cited, full 105-paper list not tabulated Partial—coarse per-system topology tags Autonomy-maturity model (5 levels); not coded per security function [8] Security and privacy of LLM agents as attack targets Narrative No No No [11] Agentic security, general (applications, threats, defenses) Documented multi-stage search (DB query + venue scan + snowballing); stated inclusion/exclusion criteria Public, continuously updated list (more than 260 papers); not per-paper coded Quantified—six paradigms with per-category counts, not broken out by security function Quantified by offense/defense; not coded per security function [12] Agentic AI in cybersecurity (SOC, IR, offense, governance) Narrative No Conceptual No Knowledge augmentation No No No No transparency Regulatory analysis No No No No This Review Eight security functions across the defensive lifecycle Documented protocol with stated inclusion and exclusion criteria Yes—59 studies, full coding matrix (Appendix B) Six patterns, single- and multi-agent, with reported performance Coded dimensions (D4, D5), per function (Section 6) Audited—vectorstore/embeddingmodel disclosure checked per study DORA, NIS2, EU AI Act This paper makes the following contributions. The taxonomy is the primary contribution; the coded corpus is the evidence base that populates and tests it. • A five-dimensional taxonomy for classifying LLM-based cybersecurity agents by security function, agent architecture, knowledge augmentation, human-in-the-loop posture, and evaluation rigor. • A PRISMA-guided review of 59 primary studies published between 2022 and 2026, spanning eight security function categories, with the full search record and per-paper coding published for inspection. We characterize this corpus explicitly as a documented subset rather than a census: an independent search of six bibliographic databases recovers 86.3% of the studies the primary search had surfaced (79.7% of the full corpus) while indicating a total eligible literature on the order of 400 studies (Section 3.1). It is the evidence base for the taxonomy, not a claim of exhaustive coverage. • A comparative analysis of agent architecture patterns, separating prevalence from performance: single-agent tool-calling dominates by count, while centralized multi-agent orchestration produces the strongest reported results from the smallest evidence base. • A structured gap analysis revealing critical underrepresentation in incident response, forensics, and compliance verification. • Identification of open research directions, including regulatory alignment under DORA [15], NIS2 [16], and the EU AI Act [17], where we document the near-absence of financial sector evidence rather than synthesizing it. The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2 introduces the necessary background on LLM agent architectures and cybersecurity taxonomy. Section 3 describes the systematic review methodology and PRISMA flow. Section 4 covers LLM agent applications by security domain. Section 5 analyzes multi-agent orchestration architectures https://doi.org/10.3390/jcp6050159 J. Cybersecur. Priv. 2026, 6, 159 4 of 50 in depth. Section 6 discusses cross-cutting challenges. Section 7 presents open research directions. Section 8 concludes the paper. 2. Background and Preliminaries 2.1. LLM Capabilities Relevant to Cybersecurity Large Language Models are deep neural networks based on the transformer architecture [18], pre-trained on large corpora through next-token prediction and subsequently aligned to follow instructions via reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) [19] or direct preference optimization [20]. Several capabilities of contemporary LLMs are particularly consequential for cybersecurity automation. Natural language understanding of security artifacts. LLMs excel at processing the heterogeneous, semi-structured text that permeates security operations: raw log streams, common vulnerabilities and exposures (CVE) and national vulnerability database (NVD) advisories, threat reports, cyber threat intelligence (CTI) feeds, and vulnerability disclosures. Systems such as LogParser-LLM [21] report a grouping accuracy of up to 0.909 on their full 14-dataset benchmark, while LogBatcher [22] processes 3.6 million log messages at a grouping accuracy (GA) of 0.972—the proportion of log messages correctly assigned to their true template group—with a reported 100% reduction in LLM inference cost through batched querying. LogPrécis [23] further demonstrates that an LLM can distil hundreds of thousands of raw log sessions into thousands of structured attack fingerprints. Code comprehension and generation. Modern code-capable LLMs understand program semantics, data flow, and control flow across multiple languages. This underpins vulnerability detection applications: IRIS [24] exploits LLM code reasoning to infer taint specifications and filter false positives from CodeQL static analysis, achieving wholerepository vulnerability detection that purely rule-based approaches cannot match. Conversely, the same capability introduces risk: LLM-generated code is repeatedly shown to contain CWE-classified security weaknesses across the primary studies cataloged by recent systematic reviews [9], though reported prevalence varies widely by study, model, and benchmark rather than converging on a single rate. Chain-of-thought and structured reasoning. Chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting [25] substantially improves LLM performance on multi-step security tasks such as attack technique attribution, exploit chain planning, and incident triage: PentestGPT [10] uses a CoT-driven task tree to guide multi-stage penetration test reasoning, explicitly invoking the CoT methodology at each planning step. Structured reasoning in this domain is not limited to CoT, however: CTI-Thinker [26] maps raw threat reports to ATT&CK tactics through a GraphRAG-based reasoning engine and semantic alignment rather than explicit chain-of-thought prompting. Tool and function calling. LLMs began invoking external tools during generation in 2023, first through self-supervised methods that learn when and how to insert API calls into generated text from a handful of demonstrations [27], and shortly after through vendorprovided function-calling interfaces that constrain tool invocations to a machine-readable, schema-defined format. In security contexts, this allows agents to invoke scanners (Semgrep, Nmap, Metasploit), query knowledge bases (MITRE ATT&CK, NVD), and interact with security information and event management (SIEM) platforms in a programmatically verifiable manner. Function calling is the technical foundation on which all tool-calling and multi-agent architectures described in Section 2.2 are built. Context window management. Contemporary LLMs support context windows of 128K–1M tokens, enabling in-context processing of entire codebases, extended incident timelines, or multi-turn analyst dialogues without external memory. However, context degradation under long sequences remains a known failure mode [6], observed empirically https://doi.org/10.3390/jcp6050159 J. Cybersecur. Priv. 2026, 6, 159 5 of 50 in PentestGPT [10] when repeated failed exploit attempts exhaust the model’s ability to reconsider prior hypotheses. Key limitations. Three limitations are particularly salient in security applications. Hallucination—the generation of plausible but factually incorrect content—is especially costly when it produces phantom CVEs, incorrect exploit paths, or false threat actor attributions. Knowledge cutoff means models may be unaware of recent vulnerabilities or threat campaigns, making retrieval augmentation essential [28]. Adversarial fragility exposes LLMs to prompt injection attacks [29], a threat that is amplified when the model operates as an autonomous agent with access to sensitive tools and data. 2.2. Agent Architectures: From Single-Agent to Multi-Agent Systems An LLM agent is distinguished from a bare LLM by the presence of an action loop: a cycle of perceiving context, reasoning over available tools, issuing actions, observing outcomes, and updating state toward a goal [6]. This section defines the six architectural patterns used as the second dimension of our classification framework (Section 3.4); Figure 1 provides a visual overview of all six patterns. SA-IO: Input/Output Input LLM Output stateless · no tools · no loop SA-TC: Tool-Calling (ReAct) Thought Action LLM Tools Obs. ReAct loop · iterative refinement SA-PE: Plan-and-Execute Planner T1 T2 T3 Executor decompose · sequence · replan MA-CH: Centralized/Hierarchical Orchestrator A1 A2 A3 router · dispatch · aggregate MA-PP: Peer-to-Peer / Pipeline out out A1 A2 A3 pipeline · no central orchestrator MA-DB: Debate/Consensus A1 A2 A3 Consensus deliberate · iterate · converge Figure 1. Taxonomy of LLM agent architectural patterns forming the second classification dimension of this review. Top row (single-agent): SA-IO—stateless input/output; SA-TC—iterative ReAct tool-calling loop (Thought → Action → Observation); and SA-PE—plan-and-execute with explicit task decomposition and replanning. Bottom row (multi-agent): MA-CH—centralized hierarchical orchestrator dispatching to specialized subagents; MA-PP—peer-to-peer sequential pipeline with no single controller; and MA-DB—debate/consensus across multiple LLM instances converging to an aggregated output. Arrows denote task dispatch, tool invocation, structured output passing, and deliberation flows. 2.2.1. Single-Agent Architectures SA-IO (Input/Output). In the simplest pattern, the LLM performs single-shot generation—classification, extraction, or synthesis—and invokes no tools and performs no iterative reasoning of its own. The model receives a structured input (a log line, a code snippet, a CVE description) and produces a structured output (a label, a severity score, a tactic, technique, and procedure (TTP) identifier). It is important to be precise about what qualifies here, because the boundary against the exclusion criteria in Section 3.2 runs through it: SA-IO systems are embedded in an external automated execution or verification loop that consumes the model’s output and, in most cases, feeds a subsequent generation. Fuz- https://doi.org/10.3390/jcp6050159 J. Cybersecur. Priv. 2026, 6, 159 6 of 50 zGPT [30] is representative—the model proposes test inputs, an external fuzzing harness executes them, and coverage feedback drives the next round. A system in which the LLM output is consumed by nothing and closes no loop at all is not an agent under our definition and was excluded under EC1. Despite its simplicity, SA-IO yields strong baselines for well-defined subtasks such as ATT&CK TTP extraction from CTI sentences [31]. SA-TC (Single-Agent, Tool-Calling). The ReAct framework [5] formalized the pattern of interleaving reasoning traces (Though [...] M malware binary MITRE ATT&CK agent 2025 C4: Financial sector, compliance, and regulation 25 “LLM” “financial cybersecurity” agent 26 “LLM” “fraud detection” agentic agent 27 “large language model” “financial sector” cybersecurity agent 28 “LLM DevSecOps” agent “secure code review” 29 “LLM” “GRC” governance risk compliance agent automation 30 “LLM” “SBOM” “supply chain security” software agent 31 Gartner Forrester LLM agents financial security 2024 2025 32 “LLM” “ISO 27001” OR “NIST” compliance automation agent cybersecurity paper 33 “EU AI Act” “financial services” “security agents” LLM implications 2024 2025 34 arxiv “LLM agent” “threat intelligence” “incident response” security operations center 2024 2025 35 “LLM” “Copilot” “secure code” vulnerability detection agent 2024 2025 paper 36 arxiv “ThreatModeling-LLM” banking threat modeling NIST 800-53 Yang 2024 37 “SEVENLLM” OR “SecureFalcon” OR “Audit-LLM” OR “HuntGPT” financial cybersecurity LLM agent paper 2024 C3: Agent architecture and orchestration 38 site:arxiv.org multi-agent LLM cybersecurity 2024 2025 39 “LangGraph” security agent cybersecurity 40 “ReAct agent” security cybersecurity LLM 41 “multi-agent orchestration” cybersecurity LLM 42 PentestGPT LLM agent penetration testing arxiv 43 “AutoGen” cybersecurity agent penetration testing 44 “retrieval augmented generation” cybersecurity CVE MITRE ATT&CK 45 “CyberRAG” OR “SecGPT” OR “CybersecurityGPT” LLM agent framework arxiv 46 VulnBot multi-agent penetration testing arxiv 2025 47 “cooperative agents” OR “debate” multi-agent security LLM SOC incident response 48 “LLM” “knowledge graph” cybersecurity agent threat intelligence arxiv https://doi.org/10.3390/jcp6050159 J. Cybersecur. Priv. 2026, 6, 159 44 of 50 Table A2. Cont. # Query as Executed C2/C3: Penetration testing, vulnerability discovery, and benchmarks 49 site:arxiv.org “LLM” “penetration testing” 2024 2025 50 “PentestGPT” OR “AutoPentest” OR “PENTESTGPT” 51 “LLM penetration testing” agent 52 “LLM vulnerability detection” code agent 2024 2025 53 “LLM” “capture the flag” CTF cybersecurity agent benchmark 54 “LLM red teaming” cybersecurity agent automated 2024 55 “Cybench” OR “EnIGMA” LLM cybersecurity agent benchmark CTF 56 “LLM fuzzing” “ChatAFL” OR “FuzzGPT” security vulnerability 57 “AutoAttacker” LLM post-breach attack agent arxiv 58 “language agents as hackers” OR “HackSynth” OR “AutoPT” LLM cybersecurity arxiv 59 site:arxiv.org “LLMDFA” OR “VulDetectBench” OR “LLMs Cannot Reliably” vulnerability detection Of the 584 results returned, 520 carried an identifiable bibliographic host. Table A3 reports their distribution. Two features of this distribution are worth stating plainly. First, arXiv accounts for the large majority of scholarly results, which reflects both the genuine preprint concentration of this literature and the ranking behavior of the search engine; it should not be read as evidence that other indexes were searched to comparable depth. Second, roughly 45% of returned results were non-scholarly—vendor blog posts, code repositories, trade press, and analyst notes—and were removed before screening. A perdatabase query protocol would not have returned these, and their presence is a direct consequence of the retrieval method. Table A3. Distribution of the 520 source-attributable results across bibliographic hosts, before deduplication. Hosts returning fewer than four results are aggregated. Host arXiv GitHub/GitHub Pages MDPI Medium ACM Digital Library Elsevier ScienceDirect USENIX SpringerLink ResearchGate MITRE Emergent Mind Semantic Scholar Gartner ACL Anthology IEEE Xplore Other hosts (<4 results each) Total Results 238 43 16 16 9 8 8 8 8 6 6 5 5 4 1 139 520 % of Attributable 45.8 8.3 3.1 3.1 1.7 1.5 1.5 1.5 1.5 1.2 1.2 1.0 1.0 0.8 0.2 26.7 100.0 Title-level deduplication reduced the 520 attributable results to 380 unique records; removal of non-scholarly items left 174 unique scholarly records, to which citation chaining added 37, giving the 211 records that entered screening (Figure 2). https://doi.org/10.3390/jcp6050159 J. Cybersecur. Priv. 2026, 6, 159 45 of 50 Appendix D. Verification Search This appendix reports the independent database search used to characterize the coverage of the primary retrieval (Section 3.1). The exact Boolean string executed against each interface, adapted to its syntax, is published with the data deposit; Table A4 summarizes the sources, execution dates, and yields. Where an interface imposed limits—ScienceDirect permits at most eight Boolean connectors, IEEE Xplore caps citation export at 100 records per file—the query or the export was split accordingly. Table A4. Verification search: Sources, execution dates, and yields. Source arXiv Scopus IEEE Xplore SpringerLink ACM Digital Library Elsevier ScienceDirect Total Queries 8 1 1 1 1 1 16 Executed 12 August 2026 13 August 2026 12 August 2026 12 August 2026 12 August 2026 12 August 2026 Hits 982 505 416 932 43 25 2903 Exported 761 505 416 932 43 25 2682 After title-level deduplication, the six sources yield 2331 unique records. Web of Science and Semantic Scholar were not pursued: the marginal contribution of additional sources had already reached zero, in that the ACM Digital Library and ScienceDirect each recover no included study that another source does not also return. Recovery. Matching was performed on DOI first, then arXiv identifier, then normalized title, and then distinctive system name; identifier-based matching is necessary here because several preprints in the corpus have been retitled in later versions. The searches recover 47 of the 59 included studies (79.7%), and 44 of the 51 that the primary retrieval had surfaced (86.3%). Table A5 lists those not recovered. Table A5. Included studies not returned by the verification search. “Chained” marks studies identified by citation chaining rather than by the query set, which are not expected to appear. Study CTIKG [54] ChatAFL [62] FuzzGPT [30] LLMDFA [63] IDS-Agent [32] KnowPhish [93] Crimson [56] Novel Defense [89] Audit-LLM [39] Enigma [102] ThreatModeling-LLM [48] MalGEN [88] Venue or Reason Not Indexed Conference on Language Modeling NDSS ICSE NeurIPS NeurIPS workshop USENIX Security—not indexed by any source searched small regional conference arXiv preprint outside the executed strings arXiv preprint outside the executed strings arXiv preprint outside the executed strings arXiv preprint outside the executed strings arXiv preprint outside the executed strings Chained yes yes yes yes yes no no no no no no no Sensitivity. Leave-one-out analysis over the six sources shows the result is dominated by arXiv: removing it reduces recovery from 86.3% to 36.5%, and arXiv alone recovers 78.8%. Removing Scopus costs two studies, IEEE Xplore and SpringerLink one each, and the ACM Digital Library or ScienceDirect none. This is a quantitative statement of the same property that motivated the primary retrieval strategy: as of the search window, this literature is disseminated first and predominantly as preprints. Scale of the eligible literature. The verification search returned 2283 records not matched to the corpus, of which 828 mention both an agent or LLM term and a security https://doi.org/10.3390/jcp6050159 J. Cybersecur. Priv. 2026, 6, 159 46 of 50 term. A random sample of 60 of those 828 was screened against the criteria of Section 3.2: 25 were eligible, a rate of 41.7% (95% CI 29–54%), with the exclusions dominated by systems using an LLM as a classifier with no agentic loop (EC1, 14 records) and by work on the security of LLMs rather than LLMs for security (EC2, 8 records). Extrapolated, this implies approximately 345 further eligible studies (CI 242–448) within these six databases, and an eligible literature of roughly 400 studies in total. The sample and its per-record decisions are published with the deposit. This estimate is the basis for the statement in Section 8 that the corpus is a documented subset rather than a census.

